# Upstream's own ignore conventions, kept verbatim. Nothing above this line is
# ours, and nothing below it removes an upstream entry to make room for a
# fork-owned one.
#
# The HarfBuzz 14.5.1 release as vendored here ships **no** `.gitignore` of its
# own -- the top level of this tree is exactly the fourteen files
# `UPSTREAM.sha256` records, verified over the whole vendored tree -- so there is
# no upstream ignore file to preserve and none is being replaced. The entries in
# the first block are upstream's *build systems'* outputs rather than upstream's
# ignore file. They are listed so that an object or archive produced by a meson,
# CMake or autotools build cannot be committed into a tree that is built with Zig
# instead.
#
# Every pattern in that block is anchored with a leading `/`, and that is
# load-bearing rather than tidy. Two upstream Makefiles ship inside the vendored
# tree -- `src/wasm/sample/c/Makefile` and `src/wasm/graphite/Makefile` -- so a
# bare `Makefile` line would ignore real upstream files, and an ignored file is a
# file that stops being a candidate for a commit. Anchoring keeps the rule about
# what a build *here* would drop in the root, and leaves the vendored tree alone.
# Both files have digests in `UPSTREAM.sha256` and `tools/check-provenance.sh`
# fails on any file under `src/`, `docs/` or `test/` that has no recorded digest,
# so this is belt and braces rather than the only thing standing between a sloppy
# ignore rule and a missing file.
/*.o
/*.obj
/*.a
/*.lib
/*.so
/*.so.*
/*.dylib
/*.dll
/*.exp
/*.pdb
/*.exe
/*.dSYM/
/*.o.d
/.deps/
/CMakeFiles/
/CMakeCache.txt
/cmake_install.cmake
/build.ninja
/.ninja_deps
/.ninja_log
/libtool
/config.log
/config.status
/config.h
/config.h.in
/Makefile
/libharfbuzz.pc
/harfbuzz.pc
/*.egg-info/
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# zig build artefacts (fork addition).
#
# These are directories rather than single files because a normal `zig build`
# creates them, and a normal `zig build` must not dirty the tree or leak into the
# package published from it. None of them is named in `build.zig.zon` `.paths`,
# so an ignored directory can never become part of the package.
.zig-cache/
zig-out/
# Zig 0.16 fetches dependencies into a project-local directory. This package
# declares one dependency and it is a relative path, so nothing is fetched; the
# entry exists so that a future fetched dependency cannot commit its tree.
zig-pkg/

# The consumer package builds inside this repository and has its own cache and
# output tree. It is ignored here so a build run from the repository root cannot
# commit it by accident.
consumer/.zig-cache/
consumer/zig-out/
consumer/zig-pkg/

# Nix and devenv working-tree artefacts (fork addition).
#
# `nix build` writes `result` (and `result-1`, `result-2`, ... on a rebuild) into
# the directory that asked for it, and `devenv` writes its resolved inputs and
# its state beside the project. None of them is named in `build.zig.zon` `.paths`,
# so an ignored path can never become part of the package either.
# `devenv.lock` and `flake.lock` are deliberately *not* here: they are real
# inputs, they belong to the driver, and ignoring them would be the wrong way to
# keep a build out of a commit.
result
result-*
.devenv/
.devenv.flake.nix
.devenv.flakes/
devenv.local.nix
.direnv/